From 1c8317840631cfe12fcdc66bf6c8086111daff29 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 21:34:10 -0300
Subject: [PATCH v2 08/13] hv: vmbus: retain buffer ownership across GPADL and
 page state

A ring or data buffer can reach release while a GPADL post may have
reached the host, its teardown has not been acknowledged, or its page
encryption state is unresolved. Retain a separately owned record until
host ownership and page state are known and mapping references are gone.

A channel-offer rescind is not a GPADL teardown acknowledgment. Neither
a host-originated rescind nor local unload clears a GPADL handle, leak
state, or retained host ownership. A matching GPADL_TORNDOWN response or
a create that could not have reached the host can clear that ownership.
Without an applicable host protocol guarantee, rescinded buffers remain
retained; this deliberately prefers retention over host-visible reuse.

The reclaim workqueue waits for folio references to return to the
allocator's own reference. It re-encrypts direct-map chunks before
freeing, and retains backing after an unknown page-state transition.
Shutdown drains reclaimable owners and preserves unresolved owners.

Introduce the owned allocation/GPADL entry points alongside the existing
interfaces. The next patch restores the external allocator prototype.

Opaque, monotonically assigned owner identifiers and trace events describe
create, release, retention, discard and reclaim without kernel addresses.
Same-sized allocations remain distinct; a release can be reconciled with
its actual owner rather than a size class.

Five KUnit cases cover the reclaim and scheduling gates, production
GPADL teardown after host and local rescind (both established handles
and possibly delivered creates), mapping references, and repeated
release. Rescind tests require -ENODEV, preserved handles/leak state,
retained host ownership, and no reclaim scheduling.

Signed-off-by: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
